<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Geshur</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 900 BCE (approx.) · capital Bethsaida</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Geshur, a small Aramaean kingdom on the north-east shore of the Sea of Galilee, tied by marriage to King David.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Geshur occupied the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golan-heights">Golan</a> and the north-eastern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-galilee">Sea of Galilee</a>. It appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> as an Aramaean kingdom of the 10th century BCE, and excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethsaida">Bethsaida</a> and Tel Hadar have revealed fortified towns of the period.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethsaida">Bethsaida</a> had a large city gate with a stele showing a bull-headed god. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Bible</a>, King Talmai of Geshur gave his daughter <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aram-maacah">Maacah</a> in marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-israel">United Kingdom of Israel</a>; their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absalom">Absalom</a> later took refuge in Geshur.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Geshur disappears from records in the 9th century BCE, probably absorbed by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aram-damascus">Aram-Damascus</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/geshur.jpg" alt="Geshur" width="320" height="450"><figcaption>Image: AnonMoos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Early-Historical-Israel-Dan-Beersheba-Judea.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 900 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bethsaida</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Talmai</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Aramaean religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>, <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Geshur</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-950&amp;focus=geshur" aria-label="Geshur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5593 982 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Geshur lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5913 1238L5927 1218"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5927 1218h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5913 1238h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5913 1238h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bethsaida. <a href="/atlas?year=-950&amp;focus=geshur">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
